       <h3>\n        <a href=\"/berserk/kentaro-miura\" class=\"entity-link\">\n            Kentaro Miura\n        </a>\n    </h3>\n\n                    <p>Kentaro Miura created Berserk.</p>\n\n                    \n\n                    \n                </div>\n            \n        </div>\n    </details>\n</section>\n\n</section>\n\n\n<section class=\"did-you-know\">\n    <h2>Did you know?</h2>\n    <ul>\n        <li>Berserk is also referenced as Berserk manga, ベルセルク.</li><li>Kentaro Miura created Berserk.</li><li>The manga is the foundation of Berserk.</li><li>The 1997 anime adapts Berserk.</li><li>The Golden Age films adapt Berserk.</li><li>Evidence around Berserk is classified as fiction on Ton-o-Lore.</li>\n    </ul>\n</section>\n\n\n<section class=\"sources\">\n    <h2>Sources &amp; further reading</h2>\n    <p><a href=\"https://berserk.fandom.com/wiki/Berserk_(Manga)\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">Fandom wiki</a></p>\n</section>","overview":"<section class=\"overview\">\n    <h2>What is Berserk?</h2>\n    <p>\n        <strong>Berserk</strong> is\n        <a href=\"/berserk/kentaro-miura\" class=\"entity-link\">Kentaro Miura</a>'s dark fantasy of\n        <a href=\"/berserk/guts-traveling-party/guts\" class=\"entity-link\">Guts</a>, the Black Swordsman, and his struggle against\n        <a href=\"/berserk/griffith\" class=\"entity-link\">Griffith</a>, the\n        <a href=\"/berserk/god-hand\" class=\"entity-link\">God Hand</a>, and\n        <a href=\"/berserk/causality\" class=\"entity-link\">causality</a> itself.\n    </p>\n    <p>\n        Trace the\n        <a href=\"/berserk/band-of-the-hawk\" class=\"entity-link\">Band of the Hawk</a>, the\n        <a href=\"/berserk/eclipse\" class=\"entity-link\">Eclipse</a>, the\n        <a href=\"/berserk/brand-of-sacrifice\" class=\"entity-link\">Brand of Sacrifice</a>, and companions from\n        <a href=\"/berserk/band-of-the-hawk/casca\" class=\"entity-link\">Casca</a> to\n        <a href=\"/berserk/guts-traveling-party/schierke\" class=\"entity-link\">Schierke</a> across Midland toward\n        <a href=\"/berserk/falconia\" class=\"entity-link\">Falconia</a> and\n        <a href=\"/berserk/elfhelm\" class=\"entity-link\">Elfhelm</a>.\n    </p>\n</section>","word_count":1285,"uniqueness_score":"1.0000"},"entity":{"id":"634db0b4-9f20-4e87-a54c-43550c7102ef","name":"Berserk","slug":"berserk","type":"topic","kind":"topic","type_label":"Topic","short_description":"Berserk resumed serialization on October 9, 1992, in the magazine . From then on, each release was given the name and the numbering reset to 1. New episodes were released every second and fourth Friday of the month.…","description":"Berserk resumed serialization on October 9, 1992, in the magazine . From then on, each release was given the name and the numbering reset to 1. New episodes were released every second and fourth Friday of the month. Volumes are still published biannually in Japan by Hakusensha (Jets Comics collection), and contain 8-11 episodes depending on the release.\n\nBerserk is translated and published by , which has released 42 volumes so far, the first of which was published on October 22, 2003. As with other Dark Horse manga releases, the Japanese reading format (from right to left) is preserved in the English release. The sound effects are left untranslated in the earlier releases, but are translated in later books starting from volume 12. The company also occasionally incorporates references to various franchises, such as Evil Dead, into the dialogue. However, the content in general remains untouched.\n\nIn Latin America, Panini Comics has published the manga series in a demi-sized (120 pages) edition in Brazil (2005), re-releasing each volume in a deluxe edition from September 2014 onward. In Mexico, Berserk has been published since 2014. In Argentina, Berserk has been published since [https://www.facebook.com/paninicomicsargentina/videos/2698917623458078/ April 2019]. Both editions are also published by Panini Comics.\n\nThe manga was introduced in 1996, first in France (Samourai Editions), then in Italy (, under its Marvel Manga &ndash; later Planet Manga &ndash; imprint) a few months later. In France, following Samourai Editions' bankruptcy, the comic series is now published by both Dynamic Visions (since 2002) and Glénat Editions (since 2004), the latter edition being printed in a larger format. The manga has also been translated and released in Germany (Panini Comics/Planet Manga). A two-books-in-one Max edition has also been published in Germany and Italy since 2006 by Panini Comics.","evidence":"fiction","universe":"berserk","aliases":["Berserk manga","ベルセルク"],"wikipedia_url":null,"image_url":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/berserk/images/9/93/Berserk_vol.1_JP_cover_%28original_1990_release%29.jpg/revision/latest/scale-to-width-down/360?cb=20210403130922","image_source":"https://berserk.fandom.com/wiki/Berserk_(Manga)","image_credit":"Fair-use identification thumbnail via Fandom. Character / franchise art © Kentaro Miura / Hakusensha / respective rights holders (as applicable). Not free or redistributable artwork.","image_license":"fair_use","image_usage":"fair_use_identification","image_alt":"Identification thumbnail for Berserk (Manga)"},"connections":[],"rabbit_holes":[],"copyright":{"theme":"berserk","title":"Berserk is not owned by Ton-o-Lore.","body":"Berserk, its characters, artwork, logos, and related intellectual property are the property of their respective rights holders, including Kentaro Miura, Kouji Mori, Studio Gaga, Hakusensha, and other credited rights holders. Ton-o-Lore is an independent, unofficial fan and informational project and is not affiliated with or endorsed by those rights holders. Identification logos and thumbnails, when shown, are low-resolution images used under a fair-use rationale for commentary and identification only — not free or redistributable artwork."},"subject":{"id":"berserk","name":"Berserk","theme":"berserk","path":"/berserk","logo":{"license":"fair_use","usage":"fair_use_identification","url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f1/Berserk_anime_logo.png","alt":"Berserk logo","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Berserk_anime_logo.png","credit":"Fair-use identification logo via Wikimedia Commons. © Kentaro Miura / Hakusensha / respective rights holders."},"categories":["comics"]}}